Migration strategy
Description**Rehost:**
lift and shiftRehosting, commonly known as "Lift and Shift," is the fastest and most straightforward cloud migration strategy. The primary goal is to move your workloads as-is, making as few changes as possible during the migration.**Replatform:**
lift and optimize

In a replatform migration, you lift an existing workload and then optimize it for the new cloud environment. For example, you can optimize an existing workload to take advantage of cloud-based microservice architecture or containers in Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE).

**Refactor:**
move and improveYou can re-engineer your application to be cloud-native, often changing the code without altering its frontend behavior or experience. For example, a refactored application can be broken up from long strings of code into more modular pieces that can better take advantage of cloud capabilities, thus improving the performance of the code.

**Rearchitect:**
continue to modernizeSimilar to refactoring, but instead of restructuring how the application’s code works, you change how that code functions to optimize the application and take advantage of cloud-native properties, such as scalability, security, and agility. One example of rearchitecting an application is to take one large, monolithic application and turn it into several independent microservices.

**Rebuild:**
remove and replaceYour rewrite an application entirely for the cloud. It is often easier to build an application from scratch than to refactor its old code to work in a cloud environment. A rebuilding strategy allows an organization to plan from the ground up, choosing which cloud-native tools and capabilities to utilize from the beginning.

**Repurchase**You move from a purchased on-premises workload to a cloud-hosted software-as-a-service (SaaS) equivalent. For example, you can move from on-premises collaboration software and local storage to Google Workspace. A repurchase migration might be a lot easier than refactoring, re-architecting, or rebuilding. However, it can also be a lot more expensive and you will not get the granular features of controlling your own cloud environment.
